00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.430 align:start position:0%
pakistan is drowning the raging monsoon  has already claimed over a thousand  lives  and still  the waters keep coming  a lot of flood water entered our village  our homes are submerged some of our  relatives have died and cattle too we  are not receiving any relief from the  government  millions of people are affected as flash  flooding has washed away entire villages  and crops that in turn has raised fears  of food shortages even as the  authorities grapple with the immediate  problems of evacuating the worst-hit  areas and providing shelter to the  displaced  pakistan is an agricultural country sin  punjab and pakistan are the most  important provinces for agriculture its  areas grow cotton and rice all these  three have been affected by floods in  the coming season the farmers won't have  seed to the next crops there isn't even  enough to feed people in the short term  july in pakistan was the wettest in 30  years the government has called it a  climate catastrophe pakistan has been  ranking in the top 10 most vulnerable to  climate change for the last couple of  years now and there and their reasons  for it include a large population and  then very eclectic topography including  rivers and the sea and then we have  glaciers this time the monsoon rains  were erratic they were unprecedented the  kind of rainfall that was received we  really couldn't have planned for it the  government couldn't have planned for it  the uk the us and others have already  contributed to a disaster appeal but  more international aid is urgently  needed  even then pakistan faces a turbulent few  months in deep and treacherous waters  matthew thompson sky news
 

